from ../testing import Testing
from ../tooling/cli/Plugins import PluginsCli
from ./Api import PluginApi
from ./Capabilities import PluginCapabilities
from ./Declaration import PluginDeclaration
from ./Runtime import PluginRuntime
from ./Sources import PluginSources

export anchor Plugins:
    description:
        Plugins make the compiler extensible: they add commands to the CLI and
        Piton files to import, without changing the compiler. A plugin is
        declared in Piton and written in TypeScript, which Piton transpiles
        and runs itself in QuickJS, so using one needs nothing installed
        beyond piton.

        The first plugin is @{Testing}: `piton test` and the test keyword are
        a plugin bundled with the compiler, and the reference for writing one.

    principles:
        Compiling stays pure. Everything that can be known about a plugin
        without running it is in its Piton declaration: its name, its commands
        and their arguments, its settings, and what it may do on the machine.
        `piton check`, `piton build` and the language server read Piton only,
        and a plugin's code runs only when one of its commands is invoked.

        A plugin may only do what it declares. It reads the compiled project
        freely, since that changes nothing, but every other effect, like
        running a program or writing a file, is a capability it has to list,
        and anything it didn't list is refused.

        Bundled and installed plugins are the same. A bundled plugin's files
        are embedded in the compiler instead of read from disk, and that is
        the only difference, so the design that runs `piton test` is the one
        an installed plugin uses.

    declaration: @{PluginDeclaration}
    api: @{PluginApi}
    capabilities: @{PluginCapabilities}
    runtime: @{PluginRuntime}
    sources: @{PluginSources}
    command: @{PluginsCli}

    notYet:
        A first version leaves out what it doesn't need yet. Plugins add
        commands; they don't yet hook into `piton build` or `piton check`, add
        renderers or diagnostics, or replace Belay. Each of these extends the
        same declaration and API, rather than adding a second way to extend
        the compiler.
